research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log-512307-+{"id":"thm-baire-category-locally-compact-hausdorff","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/08ZQ","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Baire_category_theorem"],"rationale":"Stacks Lemma 5.13.1 proves that every locally quasi-compact Hausdorff space is Baire by nested compact closures, and the second source records the locally compact Hausdorff form. The item makes Dependent Choice sufficient and explicit and routes the stagewise construction through its bridge lemma, so its statement and proof are sourced alterations. Both URLs were retrieved through the model-side web route (HTTP 200). The general-sequence citation defect is reported separately.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log-512308-+{"id":"def-one-point-compactification","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/090A","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexandroff_extension"],"rationale":"The sources give the topology obtained by adjoining one point and using complements of closed compact subsets as its neighbourhoods. The item constructs a guaranteed fresh point, works without assuming Hausdorffness, and verifies the topology inline, so it is a semantic compilation rather than an exact transcription. Both URLs were retrieved through the model-side web route (HTTP 200).","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log-512309-+{"id":"thm-one-point-compactification-properties","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/090A","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Alexandroff_extension"],"rationale":"The sources give compactness, the open embedded copy, density for noncompact X, and the Hausdorff criterion for the one-point compactification. The item packages all directions under its weak local-compactness convention and expands the standard compact-complement separation proof, so both components are sourced alterations. Both URLs were retrieved through the model-side web route (HTTP 200).","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log-512310-+{"id":"lem-the-order-topology-on-an-ordinal","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Order_topology"],"rationale":"The source describes the order topology on ordinals, including isolated zero and successor points, the Hausdorff property, and clopen basic intervals. The item chooses a particular clopen basis and proves its endpoint and limit-ordinal cases in the library's ordinal notation; the proof adapts standard order-topology calculations. The URL was retrieved through the model-side web route (HTTP 200).","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log-512311-+{"id":"thm-ordinal-spaces-and-compactness","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Order_topology","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_uncountable_ordinal"],"rationale":"The sources record compactness of successor ordinal spaces, noncompactness of limit ordinals, and that omega-one is sequentially and countably compact but not compact. The item packages these with explicit zero/limit boundaries and audits Countable Choice in the sequence and cover arguments; that detailed dependency-labelled proof is locally composed. Both URLs were retrieved through the model-side web route (HTTP 200).","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:512312:+{"id":"thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-901-introduction-to-topology-fall-2004/e319b3a36ca774261b6b8c45e11804c2_notes_c.pdf"],"rationale":"MIT's long-line notes give the lexicographic long-ray construction, compactness of closed bounded intervals, and the standard noncompact/countable-compact behaviour. The item sharpens this to closed initial segments, separates the ZF noncompactness argument from the Countable Choice deductions, and supplies its own order-theoretic proof. The URL was retrieved through the model-side web route (HTTP 200).","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log-512313-+{"id":"thm-quasicomponents-equal-components-in-a-compact-hausdorff-space","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.uchicago.edu/~dannyc/notes/point_set_topology.pdf","https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0059"],"rationale":"The university notes state that components and quasicomponents coincide in compact Hausdorff spaces, and Stacks supplies the finite-intersection separation facts used in the proof. The item expands the standard clopen-neighbourhood argument into its dependency-labelled format, so the proof is a sourced adaptation. Both URLs were retrieved through the model-side web route (HTTP 200).","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log-512314-+{"id":"rem-compactness-conventions-and-choice-ledger","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Compact_space","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Axiom_of_choice","https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/11601562"],"rationale":"The sources document the competing compact/quasi-compact convention and the standard choice principles surrounding compactness theorems. The item is a library-specific compilation of those conventions and the proof costs of the page's individual results, not an exact source statement. All URLs were retrieved through the model-side web route (HTTP 200). Its false unrestricted ambient-invariance clause is reported separately.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log-512315-+{"id":"fs-a-compact-subset-is-closed-in-every-space","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0059","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sierpi%C5%84ski_space"],"rationale":"Stacks states the true Hausdorff theorem, while the Sierpinski-space source explicitly records a compact singleton that is not closed. The item restates the familiar false generalization and supplies the two-point witness in library notation; its contradiction proof is locally composed. Both URLs were retrieved through the model-side web route (HTTP 200). The separate false ambient-invariance remark is reported as a class-(a) defect.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log-512316-+{"id":"fs-compact-implies-sequentially-compact","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sequentially_compact_space","https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/08ZU"],"rationale":"The sources record that compactness and sequential compactness need not agree outside metric or first-countable settings and that arbitrary products of compact spaces are compact. The item uses the standard uncountable Cantor-cube diagonal witness and gives its own coordinate proof, so the false statement is a semantic restatement and the refutation is local. Both URLs were retrieved through the model-side web route (HTTP 200).","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log-512317-+{"id":"fs-sequentially-compact-implies-compact","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Order_topology","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_uncountable_ordinal"],"rationale":"The sources record that omega-one with the order topology is sequentially compact but not compact. The item restates the false implication and expands the ordinal witness using the page's earlier theorem; its short contradiction is locally composed. Both URLs were retrieved through the model-side web route (HTTP 200).","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log-512318-+{"id":"fs-countably-compact-implies-compact","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Countably_compact_space","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Order_topology"],"rationale":"The sources give countable compactness and the standard omega-one example that is countably compact but not compact. The item presents that result as a refutation of the unrestricted implication and delegates the substantive ordinal proof to its earlier theorem, so the refutation proof is local. Both URLs were retrieved through the model-side web route (HTTP 200).","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
