research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log:616857:+{"id":"thm-the-long-line-is-countably-compact-and-not-compact","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://ocw.mit.edu/courses/18-901-introduction-to-topology-fall-2004/e319b3a36ca774261b6b8c45e11804c2_notes_c.pdf"],"rationale":"MIT's long-line notes give the lexicographic long-ray construction, compactness of closed bounded intervals, and the standard noncompact/countable-compact behaviour. The item sharpens this to closed initial segments, separates the ZF noncompactness argument from the Countable Choice deductions, and supplies its own order-theoretic proof. The URL was retrieved through the model-side web route (HTTP 200).","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log-616858-+{"id":"thm-quasicomponents-equal-components-in-a-compact-hausdorff-space","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-altered","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://math.uchicago.edu/~dannyc/notes/point_set_topology.pdf","https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0059"],"rationale":"The university notes state that components and quasicomponents coincide in compact Hausdorff spaces, and Stacks supplies the finite-intersection separation facts used in the proof. The item expands the standard clopen-neighbourhood argument into its dependency-labelled format, so the proof is a sourced adaptation. Both URLs were retrieved through the model-side web route (HTTP 200).","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log-616859-+{"id":"rem-compactness-conventions-and-choice-ledger","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"not-applicable","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Compact_space","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Axiom_of_choice","https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/11601562"],"rationale":"The sources document the competing compact/quasi-compact convention and the standard choice principles surrounding compactness theorems. The item is a library-specific compilation of those conventions and the proof costs of the page's individual results, not an exact source statement. All URLs were retrieved through the model-side web route (HTTP 200). Its false unrestricted ambient-invariance clause is reported separately.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log-616860-+{"id":"fs-a-compact-subset-is-closed-in-every-space","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/0059","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sierpi%C5%84ski_space"],"rationale":"Stacks states the true Hausdorff theorem, while the Sierpinski-space source explicitly records a compact singleton that is not closed. The item restates the familiar false generalization and supplies the two-point witness in library notation; its contradiction proof is locally composed. Both URLs were retrieved through the model-side web route (HTTP 200). The separate false ambient-invariance remark is reported as a class-(a) defect.","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log-616861-+{"id":"fs-compact-implies-sequentially-compact","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sequentially_compact_space","https://stacks.math.columbia.edu/tag/08ZU"],"rationale":"The sources record that compactness and sequential compactness need not agree outside metric or first-countable settings and that arbitrary products of compact spaces are compact. The item uses the standard uncountable Cantor-cube diagonal witness and gives its own coordinate proof, so the false statement is a semantic restatement and the refutation is local. Both URLs were retrieved through the model-side web route (HTTP 200).","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log-616862-+{"id":"fs-sequentially-compact-implies-compact","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Order_topology","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/First_uncountable_ordinal"],"rationale":"The sources record that omega-one with the order topology is sequentially compact but not compact. The item restates the false implication and expands the ordinal witness using the page's earlier theorem; its short contradiction is locally composed. Both URLs were retrieved through the model-side web route (HTTP 200).","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
research/audit/wave4-dispatch/audit-beta-topology.log-616863-+{"id":"fs-countably-compact-implies-compact","statement":"ai-altered","proof":"ai-generated","evidence":"semantic-source","urls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Countably_compact_space","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Order_topology"],"rationale":"The sources give countable compactness and the standard omega-one example that is countably compact but not compact. The item presents that result as a refutation of the unrestricted implication and delegates the substantive ordinal proof to its earlier theorem, so the refutation proof is local. Both URLs were retrieved through the model-side web route (HTTP 200).","alpha_concurred":false,"at":"2026-08-04"}
